 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of hidden layers and the width of the network. Rendering time scales with the number of samples per ray and the number of rays [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Constant with respect to spatial resolution, depending only on the number of network parameters [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** The ReLU-MLP is slower to converge than SIREN for high-frequency details, often failing to reach sharp reconstructions in standard training steps (e.g., 2000 steps) due to spectral bias [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Simple architecture, easy to implement, constant memory usage regardless of scene complexity or resolution.
-*   **Limitations:** Spectral bias limits high-frequency detail reconstruction; requires careful initialization and potentially more training steps compared to sinusoidal networks.
-*   **When to Use:** As a baseline for comparison, or for scenes with low-frequency content where high-frequency detail is not critical.
-*   **When Not to Use:** For scenes requiring high-frequency details or sharp edges, where SIREN or other specialized architectures are preferred [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(L \cdot H^2)$ per sample, where $L$ is the number of layers and $H$ is the hidden width. Training time is significantly longer than SIREN for high-frequency scenes; fitting an image may require ~2000 steps for ReLU-MLP compared to ~100 steps for SIREN [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(L \cdot H^2)$ for storing weights.
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement; standard ReLU activations are computationally efficient and widely supported; serves as a robust baseline for comparing spectral bias effects.
+*   **Limitations:** Poor at representing high-frequency details due to spectral bias; requires more training steps to converge on complex scenes compared to sinusoidal-based fields [S1, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** As a baseline for comparison; for low-frequency or smooth scenes; when computational efficiency of activation functions is prioritized over reconstruction fidelity.
+*   **When Not to Use:** For high-frequency details (e.g., sharp edges, fine textures); when training time is constrained.
 
 ## Background
-The MLP neural field, specifically using ReLU activations, serves as a baseline for coordinate-based neural representations [S1, unofficial]. It was introduced in the context of differentiable rendering to map continuous coordinates to signal values, contrasting with the SIREN architecture which uses sine activations to handle high-frequency details [S1, unofficial]. The method is part of the broader family of neural and primitive-based representations for volume data [G].
+The plain MLP neural field was introduced as a baseline method in early neural rendering research to contrast with the SIREN (Sinusoidal Representation Networks) architecture [S1, unofficial]. While SIREN demonstrated the ability to represent high-frequency signals using sine activations, the ReLU-MLP highlighted the limitations of standard feedforward networks in this domain, specifically spectral bias [S1, unofficial]. It is commonly used in educational implementations of differentiable rendering to illustrate the fundamental mapping from coordinates to signal values [S1, unofficial].
 
